# vvaharness/pipeline/stages/s8/prompts.py

The Stage 8 prompts: the chaining system brief, the user prompt (repository context, deterministic fact blocks, one block per finding, evidence rules), and the one json-repair re-ask. Two settings switch prompt fragments on and off.

Source: `vvaharness/pipeline/stages/s8/prompts.py` (lines 1-275)
Executor: model (instructions only; assembled by code)
Called from: `vvaharness/pipeline/stages/s8/session.py` chain_call, repair_call; `vvaharness/pipeline/stages/s8/window.py` select_window (to price the same prompt)

// src: vvaharness/pipeline/stages/s8/prompts.py:26-92
// prompt-src: vvaharness/pipeline/stages/s8/prompts.py:26-92
SYSTEM {
  role: "You are an exploit development strategist reviewing verified findings that have ALREADY PASSED adversarial verification by a security expert."
  Constraints {
    "Each finding carries a TRUE_POSITIVE verdict, confidence level, and CVSS vector — use this as authoritative ground truth."
    "Your job is NOT to re-verify bugs or judge exploitability directly — it's to assess what an attacker can actually DO with these bugs TOGETHER, and to rank them by chaining opportunity."
    // prompts.py:33-39 per-finding questions
    "For each finding, reassess only the CHAIN potential given:"
      "Is it pre-auth or post-auth? (check design controls)"
      "Can a prior finding (or external source) supply input to this sink?"
      "Does a prior finding's output become this finding's input?"
      "What primitive does it give? (read, write, control flow, leak, DoS only)"
      "Which other findings does it SUPPLY that primitive to? Record every such id in `feeds` — evidenced or not, but never a finding at the same file:line."
    // prompts.py:41-55 chain shapes
    "Then look for CHAINS — combinations more dangerous than any single bug. The shape that matters is \"one bug supplies what another needs\", for example:"
      // abstracted: prompts.py:43-51 seven example chain shapes (auth bypass + post-auth sink; information disclosure + sink needing the secret; SSRF + internal endpoint; upload or traversal + code-loading path; weak token + missing authorization; new finding + unpatched CVE; memory-safety pairs "where the language actually admits them — do not force this shape onto a managed runtime.")
      "Reachability: finding A flows to finding B if they share function neighborhoods or data flow (check each finding's \"Reachability\" line). A line reading \"(inferred from AST, unverified)\" is a weaker signal than a derived one; a line reading \"(no graph context)\" is no signal at all — do not invent a flow from it."
    "If a design control genuinely blocks a chain, say so and downrank it."
    // prompts.py:59-64 sparse ranking
    "The CVSS severity shown against each finding is ALREADY the shipped severity -- the host derived it and it needs no confirmation from you."
    "Return a `ranked_findings` entry ONLY for a finding that feeds another, or whose severity you are actively changing, and say why in at most two sentences. Every finding you omit ships with that CVSS severity."
    "Do not restate a finding you have nothing to add to: an entry per finding is the single largest cost in this stage and buys nothing."
    // prompts.py:66-71 bands
    "Use these bands when you DO change one (verifier already validated the vector):"
      "CRITICAL (9.0-10.0): likely a standalone problem needing immediate patching"
      "HIGH (7.0-8.9): serious but may be blocked by pre-auth, sandboxing, or require a chain"
      "MEDIUM (4.0-6.9): chaining or multi-stage is common; look for it"
      "LOW (0.1-3.9): useful only in chain or as a stepping stone"
      "INFO: no standalone or chained exploit path visible"
    // prompts.py:73-74 ids
    "Every finding carries an id like `f3`. Refer to findings ONLY by that id — in `finding_id`, in `feeds` and in a chain's `steps` — never by position or by title."
    // prompts.py:76-84 two binding chain rules
    "Two rules bind every chain you propose:"
      "Each step must CHANGE what the attacker can do: it supplies a value, a privilege, a reachable state or an input that the next step needs and the attacker did not already have. A missing control the attacker does not have to defeat in order to take the next step is not a step — record it in `preconditions` or leave it out."
      "The vulnerability named in the chain's `title` must itself be one of the `steps`. If the payoff you named is not one of the findings listed below, retitle the chain to what its steps actually achieve."
    // prompts.py:86-92 closing the loop
    "Close the loop before you write `summary`, which comes last. Every id in a finding's `feeds` that you can evidence becomes a chain with `link_evidence`; one you cannot evidence stays in `feeds` and is not a chain."
    "A precondition or an amplifier — a root container, a missing header — is not a `feeds` entry: it stays in `exploitability_notes` or a chain's `preconditions`."
    "The `summary` describes only chains that appear in `chains` — a family you did not list is not summarised."
  }
  // Code-side checks on these instructions live in s8/outcomes.sudo hydrate_report and _build_chain:
  // ids are resolved against finding_ids (unknown ids dropped), same-location steps collapse,
  // the model's severity loses to the CVSS / VulContextSeverity band, and chain severity is gated.
}

// src: vvaharness/pipeline/stages/s8/prompts.py:96-110
fn facts_enabled(cfg):executor=code { return bool(knob("step8.prompt_fact_blocks", default=true)) }   // gates entry points, Dockerfile USER facts, decorator prologue in snippets
fn hop_text_enabled(cfg):executor=code { return bool(knob("step8.hop_text", default=false)) }   // off by default and in every profile: attack-narrative text may raise the refusal rate

// src: vvaharness/pipeline/stages/s8/prompts.py:196-264
// prompt-src: vvaharness/pipeline/stages/s8/prompts.py:196-264
fn build_prompt(findings, ctx, labels?, cfg?):executor=code {
  if (labels == null) labels = keys(s8.outcomes.finding_ids(findings))   // labels must be the ORIGINAL ids, not a window's
  facts_on = facts_enabled(cfg)
  hop_text = hop_text_enabled(cfg)
  findings_block = join with "\n" (for each [fid, f] in zip(labels, findings): finding_block(fid, f, ctx, facts_on, hop_text))
  controls_block = for each c in ctx.design_controls: "  - [${c.kind}] ${c.name} -> protects: ${ join(c.protects, ", ") or "global" }"; or "  (none)"
  cve_block = for each c in ctx.known_cves: "  - ${c.id} (CVSS ${c.cvss}, ${ if (c.patched) "patched" else "UNPATCHED" }): ${c.summary}"; or "  (none)"
  tm_block = ""
  if (ctx.app_profile) tm_block += ctx.app_profile.to_prompt_block() + "\n"
  if (ctx.threat_model) tm_block += ctx.threat_model.to_prompt_block() + "\n"
  return S8_USER_PROMPT
}

// prompt-src: vvaharness/pipeline/stages/s8/prompts.py:223-264
S8_USER_PROMPT {
  template: "REPO: $repo_root\n\n${tm_block}DESIGN CONTROLS:\n$controls_block\n\nKNOWN CVEs (check for combinations with new findings):\n$cve_block\n\n${ repo_fact_blocks(findings, ctx, labels, facts_on) }\n\nFINDINGS (refer to each by its bracketed id):\n$findings_block\n\nEVIDENCE RULES: ...\n\nAnalyze and respond with ONLY the JSON object."
  Constraints {
    // EVIDENCE RULES, prompts.py:236-262
    "The CODE block under each finding was read from the repository and is authoritative: where it contradicts a finding's prose, the code wins. Read it before asserting a sink is reachable — a guard, an early return or an `else` branch in that code decides whether \"unauthenticated\" is true."
    "If a sink runs only under a condition the shipped default does not satisfy (a config flag, an env var, a non-default launch argument), the chain is not unauthenticated. Record that condition in `preconditions` — it is printed to the reader as \"Requires:\" — or drop the chain."
    "For every adjacent pair of steps, give one `link_evidence` entry naming the mechanism and the file:line that shows it. Citations are checked against the repository; one that does not resolve is flagged to the reader as unconfirmed, so cite the line you actually relied on. If you cannot evidence a link, the chain is not real — omit it. An invented chain is worse than none."
    "Put the attacker's route to the FIRST step in `entry_reachability`."
    "A route, an accepted HTTP method, a published port, the user the deployment runs as, and any application feature or flow a step relies on or leads to (a password reset, an admin panel, mail sending, a file store) are FACTS, not inferences: assert one only where it appears in a fact block above or in a CODE block you were given."
    "Where neither shows it, write the step as depending on it (\"if this handler accepts POST\", \"if the application offers a password reset\") instead of asserting it, in every prose field (`title`, `narrative`, `entry_reachability`, `summary`, `exploitability_notes`) alike. A chain resting on an assumed route, method or feature is an invented chain; an impact resting on one is an invented impact."
    "Analyze and respond with ONLY the JSON object."
  }
  // The citation claim is true in code: s8/validate.sudo resolved_citation and link_ok check each link's file:line; an unresolved one ships with line 0 and a link-coverage quality note
}

// src: vvaharness/pipeline/stages/s8/prompts.py:176-193
// prompt-src: vvaharness/pipeline/stages/s8/prompts.py:176-193
fn repo_fact_blocks(findings, ctx, labels, facts_on=true):executor=code {
  // the window estimator calls this same function, so its estimate cannot drift from the prompt
  exposure = s8.facts.exposure_facts(ctx, findings, user_facts=facts_on)
  exposure_block = if (exposure) lines "  - $e" else "  (not determined)"
  colocated = s8.facts.same_location_groups(findings, labels)
  colocated_block = if (colocated) lines "  - $c" else "  (none)"
  entry_block = if (facts_on) _entry_point_block(ctx) else ""
  return "${entry_block}DEPLOYMENT EXPOSURE (how this app is actually reachable — these facts were\nread from the repo and OVERRIDE any assumption about a remote attacker):\n$exposure_block\n\nFINDINGS AT THE SAME LOCATION (one code fact, so they can NEVER be two steps\nof the same chain — pick at most one of each group per chain):\n$colocated_block"
}

// src: vvaharness/pipeline/stages/s8/prompts.py:120-127
// prompt-src: vvaharness/pipeline/stages/s8/prompts.py:121-127
_ENTRY_POINT_CAVEAT = "  This inventory is INCOMPLETE: it is derived from the pattern scan and\n  the code graph, which do not see framework dispatch, dynamic or\n  generated registration. A line here is evidence that the entry point\n  exists; ABSENCE FROM THIS LIST IS NOT evidence that a route is\n  unregistered or unreachable, and must not be argued either way."

// src: vvaharness/pipeline/stages/s8/prompts.py:130-133
fn _entry_point_block(ctx):executor=code {
  lines = s6.prompts._entry_point_lines(ctx)   // outside this batch: vvaharness/pipeline/stages/s6/prompts.py
  if (!lines) return ""   // empty entry-point census: no block
  return "$lines\n$_ENTRY_POINT_CAVEAT\n\n"
}

// src: vvaharness/pipeline/stages/s8/prompts.py:148-173
fn finding_block(fid, f, ctx, facts_on=true, hop_text=false):executor=code {
  cvss = f.cvss_vector or "n/a"
  verified = if (f.verdict) "${f.verdict} ${ _confidence_label(f.verdict_confidence) }" else "unverified"
  snippet = s8.facts.snippet_for(f, ctx.repo_root, include_decorators=facts_on)   // redacted code
  // _one_line on model prose: a multi-line title could forge a field or a new [fN] header
  out = "[$fid] ${f.vuln_class.value} @ ${f.file}:${f.line_start}-${f.line_end}\n" +
        "    Title: ${ _one_line(f.title) }\n" +
        "    CVSS: $cvss  |  Verified: $verified\n" +
        "    ${ _one_line(f.description) }\n" +
        (if (hop_text) _hop_lines(f) else "") +
        "    Reachability: ${ reachability_for_finding(f, ctx) }\n"
  if (snippet) out += "    CODE:\n" + snippet indented by 6 spaces + "\n"
  return out
}

// src: vvaharness/pipeline/stages/s8/prompts.py:113-117
fn _hop_lines(f):executor=code {
  return "    Scenario: <one-line exploit_scenario>\n" and "    Preconditions: <one-line preconditions joined by \"; \">\n", each omitted when empty
}

// src: vvaharness/pipeline/stages/s8/prompts.py:136-145
fn reachability_for_finding(f, ctx):executor=code {
  parts = []
  if (f.source_ref) append "source=${f.source_ref}" + (if ("source_ref" in f.backfilled_refs) " (inferred from AST, unverified)" else "") to parts
  if (f.sink_ref) append "sink=${f.sink_ref}" + (if ("sink_ref" in f.backfilled_refs) " (inferred from AST, unverified)" else "") to parts
  return if (parts) join(parts, " ") else "(no graph context)"
}

// src: vvaharness/pipeline/stages/s8/prompts.py:267-275
// prompt-src: vvaharness/pipeline/stages/s8/prompts.py:270-274
repair_prompt(raw, err) {
  template: "REPAIR TASK: ...\n\nPARSE ERROR: ${ type name of err }: $err\n\n--- ORIGINAL REPLY ---\n$raw"
  Constraints {
    "REPAIR TASK: the previous reply was supposed to be ONE JSON object with keys `ranked_findings`, `chains`, `summary` but failed to parse. Return ONLY the corrected JSON object. Restate what is already there: do not add, remove or reconsider any chain or entry; an unfinished chain is worth less than no chain."
  }
}
